        <body>
            <div xml:id="d212">

                <pb facs="0387" n="[327]" sameAs="frus:frus1868p2/pg327"/>

                <head>Mr. Morrell to Mr.
                        Seward.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 9.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the United States,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">San José, Costa Rica,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1868-05-08"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >May</hi> 8, 1868.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> I have the honor to transmit herewith
                    copies and translations of the correspondence relative to the visit of the
                    Prussian steam sloop of war Augusta to the bay of Limon, and its object. The
                    documents consist of the letter of Captain Kinderling to the Prussian consul
                    residing here, with translation, marked respectively No. 1 and No. 2; the letter
                    of the consul to the Costa Rica government, with translation, marked
                    respectively No. 3 and No. 4; the reply of Secretary Volio, with translation,
                    marked respectively No. 5 and No. 6; and my letter to the secretary, soliciting
                    the copies, with translation of his reply, marked No. 7. These documents fully
                    explain themselves.</p>
                <p>I submit this correspondence without remark, merely observing that the refusal to
                    accede to the solicitation so flatteringly presented may be accounted for by the
                    pointed allusion to the Monroe doctrine, the principles of which are declared to
                    be “acknowledged by all America,” which is sufficiently significant.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to remain, sir, very respectfully, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>A. MORRELL.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Secretary of State, Washington, D. C.</hi></p>
                    <p>P. S.—I deem it proper to advise our representatives in the other Central
                        American states of this affair by this mail, and by the next will send them
                        copies of the correspondence.</p>
                    <p>Respectfully,</p>
                    <p rendition="#right">A. M.</p>
                </postscript>







